WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:06.330
It seems today that all you see are D-Class
in jumpsuits and contained SCPs.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:12.270
But where are those good, old-fashioned explanations
on which we used to rely?

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:16.150
Luckily, there’s SCP Explained.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:21.140
Lucky there’s a channel who positively can
talk all about those objects that make us

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.900
scream and cry...

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:29.649
Anyway, today, we’re going to be looking
at one freakin’ sweet SCP, Lois!

00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:31.359
I mean, uh…

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:32.359
Audience.

00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:36.600
So put on your best pair of green pants and
a white button-up, and watch out for any roaming

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.450
giant chickens, because we’re going to be
taking a look at one of the strangest anomalies

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:48.089
in the Foundation’s history, SCP-5981, otherwise
known as Nuke City.

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:53.800
It’s just another ordinary day in your average,
suburban home, where you live with a bumbling

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.020
idiot of a father, a caring, though slightly
aloof mother, a sister who seems to be blamed

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.379
for everything that goes wrong around here,
a baby brother who you suspect is secretly

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:09.040
a highly intelligent evil genius bent on world
domination, and your talking dog, whose sentience

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.770
you have never once questioned.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:15.620
Okay, this might not resemble your life exactly,
but it is definitely just another average

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.080
day in your life.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.250
You hear the front door ring, and you rush
to answer it.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.820
Opening the door, you see that it’s none
other than Matt, your friend who is absolutely

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.780
obsessed with Family Guy.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:31.810
The animated adult sitcom about the Griffin
family, whose combination of wacky antics,

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.270
pop culture references, and irreverent comedic
stylings is just about the funniest thing

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.950
in the world to Matt, who’s standing right
there in a Stewie Griffin t-shirt.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.470
You’re pretty sure Matt’s the biggest
Family Guy-guy in the world, and while you

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:50.660
just think the show is slightly amusing, you
kind of admire his unhinged fanaticism over

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:51.660
it.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.770
He’s seen every episode multiple times,
owns all of the home media releases, and even

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:02.090
has a multitude of Family Guy collector's
pieces and memorabilia, including a mint in

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.710
the box action figure of Peter Griffin signed
by the show’s creator, Seth McFarlane!

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.750
If anyone’s been keeping Family Guy on-air
for the past 15 years, it’s definitely Matt.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:16.700
And because he’s at your house right now,
that could only mean one thing — a new Family

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:17.700
Guy premiere!

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.090
That’s right.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.370
Ever since Matt decided he loved Family Guy
above all else, it would also mean he felt

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:27.850
it was necessary to make every single episode
airing as they first aired.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:32.480
If Matt was standing outside your door, it
meant that he wanted to watch a new episode

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.060
of Family Guy with you, like you two usually
did together every week.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:42.370
While a part of you didn’t understand Matt’s
obsession, you still enjoyed the show and

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.180
more importantly, spending quality time together
with your friend.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.180
“Hey Peter.”

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.230
He says, in his best Joe Swanson imitation.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:53.470
You roll your eyes as he waltzes into your
house without any further explanation.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:58.790
Matt, excited as ever, walks into your living
room and plops himself down onto the couch.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:03.709
As usual, he explains that a brand new episode
of Family Guy is about to air in an hour,

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:06.280
and he was wondering if you wanted to watch
it with him.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:09.930
Well, it’s not like he’s ever given you
a choice, you think.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.350
You say the same thing you always do.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:13.490
“Sure, Matt!

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:16.440
You always know what my answer to that question
is!”

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.850
As if Matt expected any other response from
you, he becomes all giddy and excited.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.940
Oh Matt, you strange, strange human being.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.840
An hour later, the episode begins.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.240
That broadway style intro kicks in and you
find yourself wanting more and more to kick

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.460
back and relax with Peter and the gang.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.190
But something is off.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.630
It’s not uncomfortable to watch or anything,
but all the while, you get the feeling that

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:44.620
this episode of Family Guy is somehow different
than all the others you’ve seen.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:50.000
The plot sounds like something out of a dream,
even for a show as strange as Family Guy.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.360
In the episode, Brian Griffin, the family’s
talking dog, begins dating a woman who he

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.340
discovers has fish-like gills on her body.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.970
She claims that she’s a mutant, and her
body has been altered as a result of exposure

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:08.099
to nuclear energy in her hometown, a place
she calls “Nuke City”.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:12.719
In spite of the woman’s appearance, Brian
continues to see her over a series of dates.

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:16.989
Peter Griffin, however, becomes obsessed with
the idea of Nuke City, having never heard

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:22.580
of it before and finding the concept of a
town in constant nuclear fallout, populated

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:29.099
by mutated civilians to be exceptionally exciting,
and he pushes Brian to stay with the girl.

00:04:29.099 --> 00:04:33.199
Over time, Brian and the rest of the cast
go to visit his girlfriend and her family

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:39.300
in Nuke City itself, which is depicted as
a half-destroyed town full of strange, wacky

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.300
mutants.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:46.240
Peter loves it so much that, in true Family
Guy fashion, he decides to break out in song,

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.069
singing a musical number about how much he
loves Nuke City, and how everyone should visit

00:04:52.069 --> 00:04:56.270
the place, and even offers detailed directions
on how to go there.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.469
As the plot progresses, the Griffins, who
are eating dinner at the mutant woman’s

00:05:00.469 --> 00:05:06.309
house, discover that their youngest son, the
evil, talking baby known as Stewie, has disappeared!

00:05:06.309 --> 00:05:10.419
Unable to find him, Brian begins to look a
bit deeper into the issue, and discovers that

00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:16.039
his girlfriend’s father, for unknown reasons,
has kidnapped Stewie, and is currently located

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:18.409
at the town’s nuclear power plant.

00:05:18.409 --> 00:05:22.490
Brian’s mind immediately jumps to the worst
case scenario, which is that Stewie Griffin

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:28.120
was kidnapped in order to be exploited for
his high intelligence and world-ending inventions.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.879
Brian rushes over to the power plant, with
the rest of the family in tow, wondering how

00:05:32.879 --> 00:05:36.819
and why he would abandon his date in the middle
of their meal.

00:05:36.819 --> 00:05:41.750
At the power plant, Brian faces off against
the kidnapper, his date’s father.

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:46.129
After a lengthy explanation of needing Stewie
to manufacture a giant nuclear explosion that

00:05:46.129 --> 00:05:51.509
would turn the entire world into mutants,
a fight between Brian and the mutant breaks

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:52.509
out.

00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:57.349
Thankfully, the dog wins, and pushes his date’s
father to his death as he falls into the nuclear

00:05:57.349 --> 00:05:58.479
reactor.

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:03.430
This triggers a meltdown and a consequential
explosion, which the Griffins narrowly manage

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.180
to escape in the episode’s final scene.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.680
Brian is thankful Stewie is safe, but laments
the loss of his girlfriend.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.770
The ending joke, however, is that the explosion
caused the residents of Nuke city to mutate

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:19.089
even further, with Brian’s girlfriend stepping
in front of the camera, slightly out of view

00:06:19.089 --> 00:06:23.509
as a gigantic sludge monster, who asks the
dog for a goodbye kiss.

00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:26.089
When the episode finishes, you’re generally
surprised.

00:06:26.089 --> 00:06:29.639
What a strange, great piece of entertainment.

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:34.800
Matt absolutely loved it, and leaps from the
couch over to your desktop computer and immediately

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.940
starts searching for guided tours of Nuke
City.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:40.699
But for some reason, this doesn’t strike
you as odd.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:42.639
Of course Matt wants to visit Nuke City.

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:44.110
You do too!

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:49.169
The episode was practically a glorified advertisement
for how cool the place is, with all of those

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:54.110
mutants walking around and the sweet feeling
of nuclear radiation permeating the air and

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:55.280
getting into your pores.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.550
You wouldn’t want to visit anywhere else
over the summer, so you sit down to start

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:00.550
looking as well.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:04.639
There’s not much you can find on the internet
about the place, but there is a link that

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:07.930
seems to be the hub for all things Nuke City.

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:11.839
A website titled, well, “visitnukecity.com”.

00:07:11.839 --> 00:07:17.289
The page looks incredibly high effort, with
all sorts of quality photos of Nuke City and

00:07:17.289 --> 00:07:18.930
its freakish residents.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:24.339
It even offered guided tours, and a shop with
tons of Nuke City merch for you to purchase!

00:07:24.339 --> 00:07:29.940
But most importantly, it had directions on
how to reach Nuke City, and they just happened

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:34.550
to line-up with the directions that Peter
Griffin himself had given in the show.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:39.089
As it turned out, Nuke City was strangely
close to where you and Matt lived.

00:07:39.089 --> 00:07:42.569
In fact, it was located just off of the interstate.

00:07:42.569 --> 00:07:47.969
You could go visit any time you wanted, and
that’s exactly what you were going to do!

00:07:47.969 --> 00:07:52.469
A few days later, you packed your bags for
an extended stay and hit the road, following

00:07:52.469 --> 00:07:54.189
the directions to a tee.

00:07:54.189 --> 00:07:59.619
Though unsure why, a part of you felt like
this was all a dream, and that you weren’t

00:07:59.619 --> 00:08:01.620
actually going to find Nuke City.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.340
But that couldn’t be, it had to be real!

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:09.080
There was a Family Guy episode about it, and
that website, and more importantly, you and

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.599
Matt both knew that it was real.

00:08:11.599 --> 00:08:16.300
Fortunately, those fears were quelled as soon
as you entered the city limits and gazed upon

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.849
the town’s greatness for yourself.

00:08:18.849 --> 00:08:21.550
There it was, Nuke City.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:26.639
Looking just as dilapidated and grimey as
it did in the episode, with its red, smog-filled

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:31.969
sky and its broken down buildings that were
all modified into tacky tourist shops and

00:08:31.969 --> 00:08:32.969
attractions.

00:08:32.969 --> 00:08:36.899
It was perfect, and you couldn’t be happier.

00:08:36.899 --> 00:08:41.759
All over the town were the mutated civilians,
with their discolored skin and cartoon-like

00:08:41.759 --> 00:08:46.519
features that made radiation poisoning look
like a fun condition to have.

00:08:46.519 --> 00:08:49.310
They seemed to be enjoying themselves, even.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:54.380
Guiding tours, operating shops, generally
having a great time living in their slightly

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.660
post-apocalyptic society.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:01.440
Over the next few days, you and Matt lived
it up in Nuke City, enjoying the strange food

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:07.060
and sightseeing the freaky landmarks, such
as the giant tower of human skeletal remains,

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:10.990
or the mutated consciousness blob that lived
in what used to be a public pool.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:15.360
You were even encouraged to feed the thing,
and toss him graham crackers from over the

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:16.360
balcony!

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:21.050
Overall, you felt like you had a great time
in Nuke City, and the episode itself couldn’t

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:24.209
compare to how fun the actual place was.

00:09:24.209 --> 00:09:29.110
You experienced things in those few days you’d
never experience elsewhere, such as the Nuke

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:34.550
Slides water park, or winning it big in Nuke
City’s many themed casinos.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.490
You even brought a few pieces of irradiated
rubble for your family to enjoy as souvenirs.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:43.480
It was an unforgettable experience, and you
planned to go back someday.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:48.440
At least, if you got out of the situation
you currently found yourself in, with your

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:53.130
entire home being ransacked by black-suited
agents and armed guards.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:54.649
That’s right.

00:09:54.649 --> 00:09:59.350
Only a few days after you had returned from
Nuke City, you woke up to the sound of banging

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:04.519
on your front door, and before you knew it,
your house was being raided by what you assumed

00:10:04.519 --> 00:10:09.470
was the U.S government, though you weren’t
exactly sure why.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:14.040
After being transported to an unknown location
and sitting you down in a strange-looking

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.860
room, the suited men started asking you questions.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.170
All of which pertained to Nuke City.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:25.430
You explained the entire story, how you and
Matt went after seeing the Family Guy episode,

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:29.920
and how you didn’t see why there was anything
strange about going to an ordinary tourist

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.089
destination like Nuke City.

00:10:32.089 --> 00:10:36.680
The suited man slides you a digital tablet,
showing you overhead security footage of what

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:42.110
appeared to be your car, exiting the interstate
on the day you traveled to Nuke City.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:48.269
Instead of traveling down the extensive exit,
your car trails off into the woods, disappearing.

00:10:48.269 --> 00:10:54.440
Four days later, it reappears in the same
spot, driving back home as if nothing happened.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:55.610
You’re shocked.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:58.029
Where was the road to Nuke City?

00:10:58.029 --> 00:10:59.459
You knew you had gone there!

00:10:59.459 --> 00:11:01.790
Heck, you even had the merchandise to prove
it.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:06.319
You glanced down at your Nuke Slides water
park shirt, pointing it out to the agent.

00:11:06.319 --> 00:11:10.389
The man got up and left the room, and you
could see him discussing something with a

00:11:10.389 --> 00:11:12.840
lab-coat-wearing woman outside of the room.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:17.290
When they come back in, it’s with a syringe
filled with a strange-looking orange liquid.

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:21.279
The man tells you that there is no such place
as Nuke City, and you have no recollection

00:11:21.279 --> 00:11:22.279
of going there.

00:11:22.279 --> 00:11:26.950
For whatever reason, his suggestion seems
strangely appealing, and the way he keeps

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:29.970
repeating those words keeps you entranced.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:33.829
When you least expect it, the woman inserts
the syringe into your back.

00:11:33.829 --> 00:11:36.810
You fall over, slamming your head into the
table.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:41.089
When you wake up, you have no memory of anything
that happened over the past week.

00:11:41.089 --> 00:11:45.170
You remember watching Family Guy with Matt,
though you can’t remember what the episode

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:50.889
was about for the life of you, and you remember
having a completely ordinary, almost boring

00:11:50.889 --> 00:11:52.000
week after that.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:57.019
You never think of Nuke City, or that episode
of Family Guy, ever again.

00:11:57.019 --> 00:11:58.540
So, what happened here?

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:03.060
Well, you, and just about every other person
in the country who watched Family Guy that

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:09.879
week were subject to an anomaly the SCP Foundation
has designated as SCP-5981.

00:12:09.879 --> 00:12:16.220
SCP-5981 is a curious case indeed, as the
anomaly itself isn’t the episode that aired,

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:21.889
but the concept of Nuke City, a fictional
U.S city located at the intersection of Route

00:12:21.889 --> 00:12:24.129
93 and 93 Alternate.

00:12:24.129 --> 00:12:28.791
The city’s fictionalized history claims
that it was founded in the early 1940s, but

00:12:28.791 --> 00:12:32.899
was subject to a nuclear strike in 1966.

00:12:32.899 --> 00:12:37.290
The attacking party varies between accounts,
with some saying it was Cuba, or the Soviet

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:43.750
Union, or even an accidental nuclear detonation
by U.S military personnel.

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:50.940
A very small percentage of accounts of SCP-5981
claimed that it was the Daevite Empire, the

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:56.400
ancient, anomalous society that had disappeared
centuries ago, who had launched the strike.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:00.819
Whatever the case, the story goes that after
the fallout, most of the city’s inhabitants

00:13:00.819 --> 00:13:06.730
not only survived the attack, but became mutated
in ways inconsistent with the reality of radiation

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:07.860
poisoning.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:14.629
Sprouting extra limbs, organs, gills, in a
beneficial way instead of a lethal one.

00:13:14.629 --> 00:13:20.899
Now, you might be thinking that SCP-5981 is
a memetic agent, and anyone exposed to Nuke

00:13:20.899 --> 00:13:26.149
City will believe that it’s a real place,
but that just isn’t the case.

00:13:26.149 --> 00:13:32.690
Most subjects exposed to SCP-5981 do not believe
that Nuke City exists, and no future consequences

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:33.720
will occur.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:39.639
It just so happens that you and your friend
Matt were particularly unlucky, and fell consequence

00:13:39.639 --> 00:13:45.970
to SCP-5981’s poorly understood side-effect,
which causes those exposed to Nuke City to

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:50.089
not question it, and fully believe that the
city exists.

00:13:50.089 --> 00:13:54.589
Those who do so, and attempt to visit the
city, will disappear from reality shortly

00:13:54.589 --> 00:13:59.410
after entering SCP-5981’s “city limits”.

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:04.430
Once they leave, they’ll reappear in reality,
with fully formed memories of visiting Nuke

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:09.029
City and possibly even some paraphernalia
related to the city, such as T-Shirts with

00:14:09.029 --> 00:14:15.399
the slogan “I survived Nuke City”, vials
of nuclear byproducts, photos and postcards,

00:14:15.399 --> 00:14:20.999
negotiable U.S currency won in Nuke City’s
casinos, or personalized rubble intended to

00:14:20.999 --> 00:14:22.920
function as a souvenir.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:27.670
Of course, those who return from Nuke City
are entirely safe, despite supposedly having

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:34.089
visited a place with as high a nuclear radiation
count as SCP-5981.

00:14:34.089 --> 00:14:38.970
Those who visit the area the town is supposedly
located in who are unaware of Nuke City will

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:44.470
instead find nothing but an abandoned Department
of Transportation building, leaving the city’s

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:49.440
existence accessible only to those who know
of it and fully believe in it.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.829
The Foundation first became aware of SCP-5981,
Nuke City, after the aforementioned episode

00:14:54.829 --> 00:14:59.699
of Family Guy aired on television, labeled
Season 13, Episode 7.

00:14:59.699 --> 00:15:05.899
Within a day, the website “visitnukecity.com”
appeared, and according to Foundation estimates,

00:15:05.899 --> 00:15:13.079
over 200,000 civilians visited SCP-5981 before
the Foundation caught on that this phenomenon

00:15:13.079 --> 00:15:14.949
was anomalous.

00:15:14.949 --> 00:15:19.589
After this, containment procedures were established,
with the road leading to the city being closed

00:15:19.589 --> 00:15:24.720
off, and a webcrawler being programmed to
remove any mention of Nuke City from the internet.

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:29.290
Anyone the Foundation could track down as
having visited Nuke City, like you and Matt,

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:34.019
were paid a visit by Foundation agents, interrogated,
and amnesticized.

00:15:34.019 --> 00:15:39.160
But most importantly, the Foundation made
sure to see what was happening behind the

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:43.660
scenes at Fox Studios, who produced Family
Guy at the time.

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:48.970
Seth Macfarlane, the executive producer and
creator of Family Guy, was given a stern talking

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:53.970
to by Foundation agents, but even then, his
interview offered very little understanding

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:59.660
of how SCP-5981 originated or why the episode
was even produced.

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:05.320
The Foundation forced him to produce a statement
that said that SCP-5981 was not real, and

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:09.790
was simply an element of fiction that the
writers had made up for the show.

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:14.100
After this, the Foundation removed the episode
from public distribution, and after three

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:19.589
months, way after the hype surrounding Nuke
City had passed, a complete and total information

00:16:19.589 --> 00:16:24.070
blackout on the episode was put into place,
essentially wiping this episode of Family

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:29.459
Guy from the minds of the general public through
the use of mass amnesticization tactics.

00:16:29.459 --> 00:16:34.399
Unlike those with Macfarlane, interviews with
the cast and crew of Family Guy revealed that

00:16:34.399 --> 00:16:40.870
they believed that SCP-5981 was entirely real,
and the episode would shine light on a lesser-known

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:42.519
tourist destination.

00:16:42.519 --> 00:16:46.180
While Macfarlane claimed that he had just
came up with the concept for the show, while

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:51.309
under hypnosis, the man revealed that he had
been approached by an individual wearing all-black

00:16:51.309 --> 00:16:55.889
robes and a cone-shaped hood prior to the
episode’s conception.

00:16:55.889 --> 00:17:01.300
This entity instructed Macfarlane to produce
an episode revolving around a nuclear town,

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:07.520
where in its climax, a resident would be murdered
by being pushed into a nuclear reactor.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:11.190
MacFarlane could not recall anything else
about this memory when brought back from hypnosis.

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:15.750
Still, the Foundation continued to monitor
for incidents revolving SCP-5981.

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:21.580
While some still tried to visit the city,
these events were far and few between.

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:27.460
A few notable events include a civilian who
did not return from SCP-5981, but whose family

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:31.880
was sent a letter from “Nuke City Hospital”
explaining that they had been killed in a

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:34.810
motorcycle accident while touring the city.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:40.270
Another involved a heavily injured civilian
returning from SCP-5981 after an altercation

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:41.740
with a street gang.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:46.750
Their blood revealed traces of irradiated,
nuclear radiation, yet no ill effects aside

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:50.430
from those received in the street gang beatdown
were noted.

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:54.960
Another incident involved the television channel
Cartoon Network receiving a tape containing

00:17:54.960 --> 00:18:00.090
an entire season of a cartoon revolving around
three mutated dogs and their lives, which

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:08.720
was being pitched by Radioactive Studios,
a company supposedly located in SCP-5981.

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:15.290
The final incident involved several U.S politicians
visiting SCP-5981 and accidentally being caught

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:20.330
in an explosion that occurred as a result
of a janitor pushing a plant worker inside

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:23.510
a nuclear reactor in the city’s main power
plant.

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:28.120
Much like what occurred in the episode, this
resulted in an explosion that destroyed a

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:31.500
massive portion of SCP-5981.

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:35.200
The Foundation could do nothing but cover
up the politician’s deaths and increase

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:37.700
security outside of SCP-5981’s entrance.

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:45.220
The case of SCP-5981 is certainly mysterious
and gives very little answers, but it’s

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:50.120
definitely one of the most unique anomalies
that the Foundation has had to deal with.

00:18:50.120 --> 00:18:53.740
Keeping true to the spirit of Family Guy,
let’s end with a cutaway gag.

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:55.300
Holy crap, viewer!

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:58.090
This is just like the time Dr. Bright visited
Nuke City!

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:04.810
Now go check out “SCP-2316 - The Bodies
in the Water” and “SCP-1472 - Multiverse

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:08.660
Strip Club” for more anomalous locations
that are brain-breakingly strange!

